. *Except for event times -2 and -3 (with group sizes 7 and 5, respectively), a
> ll other groups exhibit sufficient extrapolated statistical power (eg. (15/5)
> *100=300). Given that the group sizes for firstborn_child_female == 0 are suf
> ficient, we assume that statistical power will also be sufficient in the full
>  dataset for all groups with firstborn_child_female ==1 to detect a significa
> nt effect.
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
.         * We estimate a household fixed effects panel linear OLS regression u
> sing an event-study approach.
.         * Let's assume that the measure gender_inequality_behavior, construct
> ed from pli0043_v3, pli0040, and pli0049_v3, provides a valid measure of gend
> er inequality in the household based on behaviors (Hypothesis 3).
.         * If the household's firstborn child is a female, we expect the house
> hold to exhibit lower gender inequality (higher values indicate greater inequ
> ality).
.         * Accordingly, we run a linear panel regression with gender_inequalit
> y_behavior as the dependent variable, and firstborn_child_female, event_time 
> (and their interactions), and age_square and partner_age_square as independen
> t variables, using household fixed effects, analytic weights (phrf), and clus
> tering standard errors by household ID (hid).
.         
. *Note, modification from the PAP: Since most individuals in our sample mainta
> in stable partnerships, partner_age increases at thesame rate as individual a
> ge, creating perfect collinearity that prevents separate identification of th
> eir effects. Due to collinearity between age and partner_age in the fixed eff
> ects specification, we include only age, partner_age_square and age_square in
>  the final model. Alternative approaches such as using age difference (age - 
> partner_age) would also be problematic, as this variable becomes time-invaria
> nt for individuals with stable partnerships and would therefore be absorbed b
> y the individual fixed effects. 
.         
